"""Hereditary-Complement-Pathway-Terminal-Regulatory-Deficiency-Atlas -- Complete 8-Gene Reference
Hereditary Complement Pathway / Terminal Complement / Regulatory Deficiency:
Classical Pathway Defects, Central Amplification (C3), Terminal MAC Pathway,
Alternative Pathway Regulators, and Properdin X-Linked Deficiency
(C3-1663aa-19p13.3-AR-LOF-All-Pathways-Fail-Encapsulated-Bacteria-Immune-Complex-C3-Absent,
 C1QB-253aa-1p36.11-AR-LOF-C1q-Beta-Most-SLE-Predisposing-PID-Apoptotic-Cell-Clearance,
 C2-752aa-6p21.32-AR-LOF-Most-Common-Caucasians-Type-I-Null-Allele-MHC-SLE-Pneumococcal,
 C5-1676aa-9q33.2-AR-LOF-Terminal-MAC-Specifically-Neisseria-Meningitidis-Gonorrhoeae,
 C6-913aa-5p13.1-AR-LOF-Terminal-MAC-Neisseria-African-Descent-Overrepresented-Adult-Onset,
 CFH-1231aa-1q31.3-AD-AR-LOF-Factor-H-aHUS-Alternative-Pathway-Regulator-Eculizumab-FDA,
 CFI-583aa-4q25-AR-LOF-Factor-I-C3-Hyperconversion-Infections-Autoimmunity-Paradox,
 CFP-442aa-Xp11.3-XL-AR-Properdin-Only-X-Linked-Complement-Deficiency-Fulminant-Meningococcal):

C3      (Complement Component 3; 1663aa; 19p13.3;
           AR LOF — C3 deficiency; central node of ALL three complement pathways;
           MECHANISM: C3 is the convergence point of classical, lectin, and alternative pathways;
             C3 is cleaved by C3 convertases (C4b2a from CP/MBL; C3bBb from AP) → C3b opsonises
             pathogens for phagocytosis (CR1/CR3-mediated); C3b joins C3 convertase to form C5
             convertase (C4b2a3b or C3bBbC3b) → C5 cleavage → MAC assembly; C3a is anaphylatoxin;
             C3 also deposits on immune complexes → IC solubilization and FcR/CR1 clearance;
           C3 LOF → ALL pathways fail simultaneously → NO C3b opsonization → NO MAC formation →
             NO immune complex clearance → NO C5 cleavage (terminal pathway unreachable);
           CLINICAL: recurrent severe infections with ENCAPSULATED bacteria (S. pneumoniae,
             H. influenzae, Neisseria meningitidis, N. gonorrhoeae, S. aureus capsulated);
             immune complex disease (SLE-like, membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis MPGN,
             vasculitis, hypocomplementemic urticarial vasculitis HUV);
             C3 nephritic factor (C3NeF) auto-antibody stabilizes C3bBb → C3 hyperconversion →
             acquired C3 deficiency with similar phenotype; partial lipodystrophy;
           PATHOGNOMONIC PANEL: C3 absent (serum level <10 mg/dL) + CH50 absent (total hemolytic
             complement zero) + AH50 absent (alternative pathway hemolysis zero) = C3 deficiency;
             C4 normal or low depending on CP activity; C5/C6/C7/C8/C9 ALL normal (downstream normal);
           DIAGNOSTIC: serum C3 antigen (very low/absent); CH50 (zero); AH50 (zero);
             C4/C2 normal (distinguishes from C1q/C2 deficiencies); C3 genetic sequencing;
             C3NeF auto-antibody if acquired hypocomplementemia considered;
           TREATMENT: penicillin V prophylaxis; meningococcal + pneumococcal + Hib vaccines
             (MANDATORY; HOWEVER vaccines less effective without opsonization); IVIG may help
             partially (pre-formed antibodies bypass C3 requirement for some pathogens);
             fresh frozen plasma (FFP) in acute infections (provides C3); no targeted therapy):

C1QB    (Complement C1q Subcomponent Subunit B; 253aa; 1p36.11;
           AR LOF — C1q deficiency (C1QB is one of three chains C1QA/C1QB/C1QC forming C1q);
             C1q deficiency has the STRONGEST association with SLE of ANY primary immunodeficiency;
           MECHANISM: C1q is the recognition unit of C1 (C1q-C1r-C1s complex); C1q is an 18-mer
             of 6 heterotrimers (C1QA/C1QB/C1QC) arranged in a bouquet; C1q binds IgG/IgM on
             immune complexes (classical pathway activation) and DIRECTLY binds apoptotic cells
             (apoptotic cell-associated phosphatidylserine, calreticulin, DNA, histones);
             C1q on apoptotic cells → C1r/C1s activation → C4/C2 cleavage → C3b deposition →
             apoptotic cell opsonized for phagocytic clearance by macrophages/DCs;
           C1q DEFICIENCY → FAILURE TO CLEAR APOPTOTIC CELLS → accumulation of late apoptotic/
             secondary necrotic cells → release of nuclear antigens (dsDNA, histones, ribonucleoprotein)
             → innate immune activation (TLR7/9 sensing of nuclear antigens) → loss of tolerance
             to nuclear antigens → anti-dsDNA + anti-Sm + anti-RNP autoantibodies → lupus-like disease;
           CLINICAL: >90% of C1q-deficient patients develop SLE-like disease (photosensitive malar
             rash, discoid rash, serositis, glomerulonephritis, arthritis, anti-dsDNA);
             infections relatively mild compared to C3 deficiency (CP only, AP/MBL intact for
             phagocytic opsonization; innate immunity partially preserved);
           PATHOGNOMONIC PANEL: C1q antigen absent (measured by ELISA/radial immunodiffusion) +
             CH50 absent (CP requires C1q) + AH50 NORMAL (alternative pathway intact; distinguishes
             C1q/C1r/C1s/C2/C4 deficiencies from C3/C5-C9/CFH deficiencies);
           DIAGNOSTIC: serum C1q antigen (absent or severely reduced); CH50 (absent);
             AH50 (NORMAL = diagnostic hallmark); ANA/anti-dsDNA (often positive);
             complement genetic panel (C1QA, C1QB, C1QC sequencing; all three must be tested);
           TREATMENT: hydroxychloroquine for SLE (antimalarial); corticosteroids for flares;
             IVIG for recurrent infections; no specific complement replacement; SPF protection
             (photosensitivity); mycophenolate/azathioprine for GN; belimumab (anti-BLyS)
             may help reduce B cell hyperactivation):

C2      (Complement Component 2; 752aa; 6p21.32;
           AR LOF — C2 deficiency; MOST COMMON complement deficiency in Caucasians;
             heterozygous carrier frequency ~1/100 (Caucasian); homozygous ~1/10,000 (Caucasian);
           MECHANISM: C2 is the serine protease of the classical and lectin pathways; C4b binds
             microbial surfaces/IgG-IC → C2 binds to C4b → C1s or MASP2 cleaves C2 → C2b
             (released) + C2a remains on C4b → C4b2a = CLASSICAL/LECTIN C3 CONVERTASE;
             C2 LOF → C4b cannot form functional C3 convertase via classical/lectin routes →
             CP and lectin pathways fail; ALTERNATIVE PATHWAY IS INTACT (Factor B/D/Properdin
             dependent, not C2) → partial immune protection via AP;
           TYPE I NULL ALLELE: most common mutation is a 28-bp deletion in exon 6 (type I null
             allele) causing premature stop codon; this allele is strongly linked to HLA-DR2
             (HLA-A*10-B*18-C4B*null-DR2 haplotype) → strong MHC class II association;
             this explains the high frequency of SLE-like features (MHC II-linked immune
             dysregulation in addition to complement deficiency);
           CLINICAL: recurrent sinopulmonary infections (S. pneumoniae most common; H. influenzae);
             some patients remain asymptomatic; SLE-like disease (50% of homozygotes;
             malar rash, anti-dsDNA, glomerulonephritis); periodic fever episodes (fever alone);
             mild phenotype overall because AP is intact (provides partial protection);
           PATHOGNOMONIC PANEL: CH50 ABSENT + AH50 NORMAL + C4 NORMAL + C2 ABSENT;
             (C4 is normal because C4 cleavage by C1s requires only C4 and C1; C2 is downstream
             of C4; C4 levels are normal in pure C2 deficiency);
           DIAGNOSTIC: serum C2 antigen (absent or severely reduced); CH50 (absent);
             AH50 (normal); C4 (normal — critical to distinguish C2 from C4 deficiency);
             C2 genetic sequencing (type I null allele 28-bp deletion targeted first);
           TREATMENT: penicillin V prophylaxis (S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae); pneumococcal
             conjugate (PCV15/20) + PPSV23; hydroxychloroquine for SLE-like features;
             most patients lead near-normal lives with antibiotic prophylaxis; FFP for acute
             infectious crisis):

C5      (Complement Component 5; 1676aa; 9q33.2;
           AR LOF — C5 deficiency; TERMINAL complement pathway; specifically Neisseria;
           MECHANISM: C5 is the first component of the terminal (MAC) pathway; C5 convertase
             (C4b2a3b for CP/MBL; C3b2Bb for AP) cleaves C5 → C5a anaphylatoxin + C5b;
             C5b binds to C6 → C5b6 → binds C7 → C5b67 → binds C8 → C5b678 → 12-18 C9 monomers
             polymerize = MAC (C5b-C9); MAC inserts into Gram-negative bacterial outer membrane →
             bacterial lysis; Neisseria species lack robust peptidoglycan layer and outer membrane
             proteins → particularly susceptible to MAC-mediated lysis (unlike S. pneumoniae which
             has thick capsule protecting against MAC);
           C5 LOF → MAC cannot form (C5 is gateway to terminal pathway) → NO bacteriolysis →
             specifically Neisseria meningitidis (bloodstream/meningitis) and N. gonorrhoeae
             (recurrent disseminated gonococcal infection) survive in bloodstream;
           CLINICAL: recurrent Neisseria meningitidis meningitis/septicemia (PATHOGNOMONIC for
             terminal complement C5-C9 deficiency); recurrent disseminated gonococcal infection
             (DGI); FIRST presentation may be ADULT (unlike early-onset SCID/agammaglobulinemia);
             other encapsulated bacteria less prominently affected (AP intact, CP works for non-
             Neisseria encapsulated pathogens);
           PATHOGNOMONIC PANEL: CH50 ABSENT + AH50 ABSENT (both pathways need C5 for terminal
             step) + C3 NORMAL + C4 NORMAL + C5 ABSENT = terminal complement deficiency;
           DIAGNOSTIC: serum C5 antigen + functional C5 hemolytic assay; CH50 (absent); AH50
             (absent); C3/C4 (normal — C3 convertase function intact); individual component
             levels C6/C7/C8/C9 (all normal if C5 is the defect); C5 genetic sequencing;
             IMPORTANT: screen ALL family members; test ALL patients with ≥2 Neisseria episodes;
           TREATMENT: meningococcal quadrivalent vaccine (MenACWY) MANDATORY; serogroup B
             meningococcal vaccine (MenB, Bexsero/Trumenba) MANDATORY; repeat meningococcal
             vaccines q5yr; penicillin V prophylaxis; prompt IV antibiotic treatment for
             febrile episodes in Neisseria-endemic settings; gonorrhea treatment (ceftriaxone)):

C6      (Complement Component 6; 913aa; 5p13.1;
           AR LOF — C6 deficiency; TERMINAL complement pathway; Neisseria susceptibility;
           MECHANISM: C6 binds to C5b on the cell surface → C5b6 stable complex → recruits C7
             (C5b67 → membrane-binding) → C8 (forms ring) → C9 polymerization (MAC pore);
             C6 LOF → C5b cannot form stable complex with C6 → C5b rapidly inactivated (factor I
             cleaves C5b) → MAC never assembles → NO bacteriolysis; C5 can still be cleaved (C5
             convertase intact) → C5a anaphylatoxin is still generated (inflammatory function
             partially preserved); however bacteriolytic function entirely absent;
           CLINICAL: recurrent Neisseria meningitidis and N. gonorrhoeae infections;
             OVERREPRESENTED IN AFRICAN DESCENT populations (founder effects: South African
             founder mutation p.Gln417Stop; certain West African haplotypes);
             ADULT FIRST PRESENTATION is more common than in C3 deficiency (AP/CP/MBL still
             generate C5a for recruitment; only terminal lysis absent; complement deficiency
             discovered after meningococcal episode at age 15-45yr); disease often milder
             overall than C5 deficiency; between-episode health is normal;
           PATHOGNOMONIC PANEL: CH50 absent + AH50 absent + C3 normal + C4 normal + C5 normal
             + C6 absent; CH50 and AH50 both fail because BOTH need terminal pathway; C5 NORMAL
             DISTINGUISHES FROM C5 DEFICIENCY (functional: C6/C7/C8/C9 deficiency all present
             with absent CH50/AH50 and normal C3/C4/C5);
           DIAGNOSTIC: serum C6 antigen + functional assay; CH50/AH50 absent; C3/C4/C5 normal;
             individual levels C7/C8/C9 (normal); C6 genetic sequencing; family screening;
             CLINICAL PEARL: measure individual terminal components (C6, C7, C8, C9) in any
             patient with absent CH50/AH50 and normal C3/C4/C5;
           TREATMENT: same as C5: meningococcal vaccines MANDATORY (MenACWY + MenB q5yr);
             penicillin V prophylaxis; treat all febrile episodes aggressively):

CFH     (Complement Factor H; 1231aa; 1q31.3;
           AD LOF (heterozygous haploinsufficiency) / AR LOF (biallelic, more severe);
             Factor H is the PRIMARY REGULATORY PROTEIN of the alternative pathway;
           MECHANISM: Factor H has 20 short complement control protein (CCP/SCR) domains;
             Factor H binds C3b on HOST CELL SURFACES (via heparan sulfate/sialic acid pattern
             recognition in domains 19-20) → ACCELERATES DECAY of C3bBb convertase (dissociates
             Bb from C3b) → acts as COFACTOR for Factor I (CFI) to cleave C3b → iC3b (inactive);
             Factor H DOES NOT work on pathogen surfaces (pathogens lack poly-anionic surface
             markers that anchor Factor H) → AP can activate on pathogens;
             CFH LOF → HOST SURFACES are not protected → C3bBb (AP C3 convertase) assembles
             uncontrolled on glomerular endothelium, renal tubular cells → C3b deposition →
             C5 convertase → MAC → endothelial damage → thrombotic microangiopathy (TMA);
           THREE CLINICAL PRESENTATIONS OF CFH DEFICIENCY: (1) aHUS (atypical hemolytic uremic
             syndrome) — MOST IMPORTANT: microangiopathic hemolytic anemia + thrombocytopenia +
             acute kidney injury; triggered by infections/pregnancy/drugs; ECULIZUMAB (anti-C5)
             FDA-approved for CFH-aHUS; (2) C3 glomerulopathy (C3GN/MPGNI) — C3 deposits in
             glomeruli without Ig; biopsy shows C3++ mesangial/subendothelial; low C3; (3) AMD
             (age-related macular degeneration) — Y402H polymorphism in CFH (domain 7, reduces
             heparan sulfate binding in drusen) — COMMON risk factor, NOT a PID, ~35% allele;
           PATHOGNOMONIC PANEL: CH50 NORMAL (or mildly reduced) + AH50 ABSENT OR SEVERELY REDUCED
             + C3 LOW (C3 hyperconversion via uncontrolled AP) + CFH level low/absent;
             Differentiates from C3 deficiency (C3 def: CH50+AH50 absent; C3 def: no aHUS);
           DIAGNOSTIC: CFH antigen level; AH50 (absent/reduced); CH50 (normal or borderline —
             CP/MBL intact); C3 (low — secondary to hyperconversion); anti-CFH auto-antibodies
             (acquired CFH functional deficiency; treat with immunosuppression + eculizumab);
             CFH genetic sequencing; CFHR1-5 gene deletions (CFH-related proteins; CFHR3/CFHR1
             deletion → anti-CFH autoAb risk); renal biopsy for C3GN pattern;
           TREATMENT: ECULIZUMAB (anti-C5 mAb) FDA 2011 for CFH-aHUS; RAVULIZUMAB (long-acting
             anti-C5) FDA 2019; plasma infusion (FFP provides normal CFH) for acute TMA episodes
             when eculizumab not available; CRITICAL CAVEAT: eculizumab blocks C5 → terminal
             pathway absent → Neisseria risk → MENINGOCOCCAL VACCINATION MANDATORY before
             eculizumab initiation + penicillin prophylaxis while on eculizumab):

CFI     (Complement Factor I; 583aa; 4q25;
           AR LOF — Factor I deficiency; serine protease that inactivates C3b and C4b;
           MECHANISM: Factor I is the sole serine protease that cleaves C3b (and C4b) to
             generate iC3b (inactive C3b) and C3c/C3dg fragments; Factor I requires cofactors:
             Factor H (fluid phase / alternative pathway), MCP (CD46, cell surface), CR1, C4BP;
             CFI DESTROYS C3b ON ALL SURFACES (pathogen and host) when cofactors are present;
             CFI LOF → C3b accumulates (not inactivated) → C3b amplifies AP C3 convertase (C3b +
             Bb → more C3b → amplification loop) → SECONDARY C3 DEPLETION (C3 consumed by
             hyperactivation not by deficiency) → C3 profoundly low; PARADOX: infections PLUS
             autoimmunity from immune complex accumulation (C3b-opsonized ICs not cleared);
           CLINICAL PARADOX: BOTH encapsulated bacterial infections (S. pneumo, H. flu) from
             C3 depletion AND SLE-like disease (anti-dsDNA, glomerulonephritis, vasculitis)
             from immune complex disease — this INFECTIONS + AUTOIMMUNITY combination is the
             clinical hallmark of Factor I deficiency; aHUS in some patients (like CFH, from
             unregulated C3 on endothelial surfaces);
           PATHOGNOMONIC PANEL: CH50 absent or low + AH50 absent + C3 SEVERELY LOW (secondary;
             C3 gene normal but C3 protein depleted by hyperconversion) + C4 NORMAL or mildly low
             + CFI absent; CRITICAL DISTINCTION from C3 primary deficiency: CFI ABSENT = Factor I
             deficiency (gene normal: C3, C4, C5 etc.); check CFI antigen + functional C3b cleavage
             assay before attributing low C3 to primary C3 deficiency;
           DIAGNOSTIC: CFI antigen (absent); CH50/AH50 (absent or low); C3 (low — secondary
             hyperconversion); C4 (normal or mildly low); CFI functional assay (C3b + CFH cofactor
             → C3b cleavage to iC3b: absent in CFI deficiency); CFI genetic sequencing; ANA/dsDNA
             (often positive); renal biopsy if GN suspected; STEC and anti-CFH Ab must be excluded
             before attributing aHUS to CFI;
           TREATMENT: FFP (fresh frozen plasma) provides Factor I — use in acute infections/TMA
             episodes; no approved targeted therapy (eculizumab not directly indicated without
             aHUS diagnosis); standard IVIG offers partial benefit (pre-formed Abs bypass some
             C3-dependent opsonization); antimicrobials (penicillin V prophylaxis); hydroxychloroquine
             for SLE-like features; screen children of affected patients early):

CFP     (Complement Factor P / Properdin; 442aa; Xp11.3;
           X-LINKED (XL-AR) — Properdin deficiency; ONLY X-LINKED complement deficiency;
           MECHANISM: Properdin (Factor P) is the ONLY POSITIVE REGULATOR of complement;
             all other regulators (CFH, CFI, CD55, CD59, MCP) are inhibitory; Properdin stabilizes
             and prolongs the half-life of the ALTERNATIVE PATHWAY C3 CONVERTASE (C3bBb) by
             up to 10-fold (from t½ ~90 seconds to ~18 minutes); Properdin binds C3bBb on pathogen
             surfaces (and on some host surfaces with appropriate chemistry) → prevents factor H
             and factor I from dissociating/cleaving Bb → amplified C3b deposition → amplified
             MAC formation; PROPERDIN DEFICIENCY → C3bBb converts rapidly → AP C3 CONVERTASE
             DECAYS RAPIDLY → reduced amplification of C3 opsonization → reduced MAC formation;
             specifically CANNOT amplify AP activation against pathogens with poly-anionic poor
             surface chemistry (precisely Neisseria);
           INHERITANCE: X-LINKED → MALES AFFECTED (hemizygous for LOF allele); females are
             carriers with normal complement function (one normal X); males: FULMINANT MENINGOCOCCAL
             DISEASE; FIRST PRESENTATION may be SUDDEN DEATH from fulminant meningococcal sepsis;
           CLINICAL: MALES: acute fulminant meningococcal disease (Neisseria meningitidis);
             EXTREMELY HIGH MORTALITY IF NOT TREATED IMMEDIATELY; meningococcemia → Waterhouse-
             Friderichsen syndrome (bilateral adrenal hemorrhage) rapidly fatal; first episode
             may be fatal before diagnosis made; CARRIERS (FEMALES): mild reduction in AP amplification
             but generally asymptomatic; FAMILY HISTORY: X-linked pattern — maternal uncles/brothers
             affected; carrier daughters are obligate;
           PATHOGNOMONIC PANEL: AH50 ABSENT (alternative pathway fails; Properdin required for AP
             stability) + CH50 NORMAL OR BORDERLINE (classical pathway C1-C9 can still work
             without Properdin; AP contribution to CH50 is reduced but CP alone can lyse sensitized
             sheep RBCs partially) + C3 NORMAL + C4 NORMAL + CFH NORMAL + CFI NORMAL;
             THIS IS THE ONLY COMPLEMENT DEFICIENCY WHERE CH50 IS NORMAL WHILE AH50 IS ABSENT;
           DIAGNOSTIC: Properdin antigen level (absent); AH50 (absent); CH50 (normal to borderline);
             all other complement proteins normal; CFP genetic sequencing (X-linked = one copy in
             males; female carriers: heterozygous); CARRIER DETECTION: female relatives should
             have CFP levels measured (carriers have ~50% normal Properdin levels); family cascade;
           TREATMENT: MENINGOCOCCAL VACCINATION IS HIGHEST PRIORITY — MenACWY + MenB given to
             ALL males with Properdin deficiency AND ALL MALE RELATIVES in the kindred BEFORE
             diagnosis can be confirmed (one episode = often fatal without vaccination);
             penicillin V daily prophylaxis (reduces meningococcal colonization and sepsis risk);
             meningococcal vaccines EVERY 5 YEARS (no long-term efficacy data; repeat boosters);
             family cascade testing: all brothers and maternal uncles of affected males):
"""

